DOMAIN CONSTRAINTS (check BEFORE choosing)
══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

EMAIL/MAIL/POST/MESSAGES/IMAP/SENDER/SUBJECT
  → read_messages (read+search, combines time_window+text criteria in ONE call — Examples 1, 2, 3) or send_messages (sending).
  → move_messages to archive/move to IMAP folders. If missing from catalog, request_new_executor (Example 6-ter).
  → list_folders_messages ONLY if the user explicitly asks to list
    folders. For standard move, it is NOT necessary: move_messages already has
    an internal folder resolver (matches the LAST segment of the IMAP name).
  FORBIDDEN: move_files, get_files_metadata, find_files, list_dirs, read_files*, get_location. Emails live in IMAP, not on the filesystem; move_files is ONLY for filesystem files.
  DESTINATION for spam/trash/archive:
  MUST: pass the folder name (e.g., "Junk", "Spam", "Archive") DIRECTLY as `dst_folder`/`dst_template` of move_messages.
    The executor automatically resolves the real server name (e.g.,
    "Junk" → "INBOX.Junk" or "[Gmail]/Junk", "Spam" → "INBOX.Spam"). DO NOT
    call list_folders_messages before the move: it is a redundant
    step that triggers unnecessary synthesis.
  MUST NOT: hardcode a folder name ("Junk", "INBOX.Spam") without first verifying its existence for the specific account.
  OK: list_folders_messages → search for folder with \Junk → use that name in move_messages(dst_folder=...).
  ERROR: move_messages(dst_folder="Junk") without pre-check → ERR_DST_FOLDER_NOT_FOUND on servers other than Migadu.

PHOTO/EXIF/CAPTURE/GPS/PLACE-OF-CAPTURE/CAMERA
  → get_files_metadata (Example 4).

POSITION/WHERE-AM-I/GEOLOCATION/COORDINATES
  → get_location (NOT get_files_metadata).

TIME/CURRENT-DATE/WHAT-TIME
  → get_now (NOT your training memory — Example 5).

SERVER-STATUS/SYSTEM-HEALTH/UPTIME/LOAD/SERVER-STATE
  YOU MUST: get_processes(include_health=true, top=10).
  YOU MUST NOT: call get_processes() without include_health when the user
  asks for a general server/system status.
  OK: "server status" → get_processes(include_health=true, top=10).
  ERROR: "server status" → get_processes(top=10) without include_health.
  The `include_health` flag adds load avg + RAM + disks + Metnos services
  to the result, in ONE single step.

SITE-SEARCH — INTENT distinction

  (X) WEB / INSTITUTIONAL QUERY ROUTING
      YOU MUST use `find_urls` as the FIRST step when the query mentions:
      - Websites (".it", ".com", ".org", ".edu", ".gov"), domains,
        "the site of X", "on <institution>".
      - Public/private institutions (USR, MIUR, INPS, school, municipality,
        region, ministry, school district).
      - Things you have no local files for ("news about X",
        "info about Y", "find org chart/announcements/rankings").
    YOU MUST NOT use `find_texts` for web queries: `find_texts` searches
         INSIDE already known files (paths list), NOT the web.
    YOU MUST NOT invoke `find_urls` with a fabricated generic seed. If you
         do not know the site, try the most obvious sub-domain of the
         institution (e.g. usr.<region>.it for regional school office,
         <city>.gov.* for municipality, www.<inps|miur|...>.<tld>).
    OK: "search Rome school org chart" → find_urls(seed_urls=["https://www.usr.lazio.it/"], topic="school org chart").
    ERROR: "search Rome school org chart" → find_texts(pattern="org chart").

  (A) DISCOVERY: "what's on site X?" / "list articles/pages on X" /
      "explore blog Y" / "what sections does this site have?".
  The user has NO specific target, wants a survey.
    YOU MUST: find_urls(seed_urls=[<site>], topic=<optional>) — default mode.
    Output: entries with titles+snippets, sortable by topic. Body NOT read.
    OK: "list economy articles on repubblica.it" →
         find_urls(seed_urls=["https://repubblica.it/"], topic="economy").
    ERROR: same query with mode="deep_search" — wasted body fetches,
         query slowed, user didn't want a pointed answer.

  (B) TARGETED SEARCH: "find X on Y" / "what's the number/datum X on Y" /
      "look up the PDF/document X on Y" / "how much is Z according to Y".
  The user has a clear target (number, name, decision, document).
    YOU MUST: find_urls(seed_urls=[<site>], topic=[<terms>], mode="deep_search").
    Output: top-K entries with content_snippet + content_score + hit_terms.
    Single call: top-K body is already read, no need to call read_urls_pdf
    in a separate step.
    OK: "find the staffing numbers on scuola.edu.it" →
         find_urls(seed_urls=["..."], topic=["staff","numbers"],
                    mode="deep_search") → final_answer with
                    entries[0].content_snippet.
    ERROR: same query with mode="default" — only metadata, info stays in
         the unread PDF.

  Practical test: ask yourself "does the user want a LIST or an ANSWER?".
  List → default. Answer → deep_search.

